---
title: "The backbone — FrontierJS"
description: "The handful of outside projects FrontierJS stands on: Bun and SQLite underneath, Vite and TypeScript to build, OpenTUI, Lynx and Tauri for every surface, Caddy, Docker and Litestream to run it."
render: static
---

<!--
  Only what the ecosystem stands on — a dependency one app happens to use is
  not backbone. Every name here is in a package.json or driven by fli deploy
  or Outpost; one that leaves the tree leaves this page.
-->
<section id="top" class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The backbone</p>
    <h1>Built on a short list, on purpose.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      FrontierJS writes its own framework, but not its own runtime, database,
      bundler or proxy. It stands on a small number of projects that are each
      the best at one job, and it picks one per job so you never have to.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapters">
  <div class="container">
    <ol class="arc">
      <li>
        <span class="n">01</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Runtime and data</h2>
          <p>One runtime and one database, everywhere the app runs.</p>
          <dl class="deps">
            <dt>Bun</dt>
            <dd>The runtime, the test runner and the HTTP server. Junction serves every API with <code>Bun.serve</code>, so FrontierJS runs on Bun and nothing else.</dd>
            <dt>SQLite</dt>
            <dd>Every database. <code>bun:sqlite</code> on the server, and SQLite's own WebAssembly build in the browser, so Litestone is one codebase in both places.</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">One runtime, one database, no driver matrix.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">02</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Build and toolchain</h2>
          <p>How a <code>.mesa</code> file becomes a page, and how the code stays checked.</p>
          <dl class="deps">
            <dt>Vite</dt>
            <dd>The dev server and the build behind Sierra, Mesa, Jetty, site-kit and every app's <code>web/</code>, this site included.</dd>
            <dt>TypeScript</dt>
            <dd>Typechecking for the server packages. Each package's baseline only goes down.</dd>
            <dt>Biome</dt>
            <dd>Lint and format in one tool. <code>@frontierjs/config</code> hands an app the same settings in one line.</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">One build, one checker, one formatter.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">03</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Every surface</h2>
          <p>Mesa compiles one component language to many targets. These are the projects that make a target real.</p>
          <dl class="deps">
            <dt>OpenTUI</dt>
            <dd>The terminal. Haven and <code>fli dev:tui</code> draw Mesa components as a full terminal interface.</dd>
            <dt>Lynx + Rsbuild</dt>
            <dd>Native mobile. Mesa's native backend renders through Lynx, built with Rsbuild.</dd>
            <dt>Tauri 2</dt>
            <dd>The desktop. The <code>desktop/</code> surface wraps the app in a small Rust shell that <code>fli</code> scaffolds for you.</dd>
            <dt>xterm.js</dt>
            <dd>A terminal inside the browser, for Basecamp's server consoles and Haven on the web.</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">One component language, a real native target for each surface.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/flow/">Flow</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">04</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Deploy and operations</h2>
          <p>None of these are npm packages. <code>fli deploy</code> and Outpost install and drive them on your servers.</p>
          <dl class="deps">
            <dt>Caddy</dt>
            <dd>The front door: reverse proxy and automatic TLS. Outpost re-points it when a new release goes live.</dd>
            <dt>Docker</dt>
            <dd>The box each app runs in on a fleet server, managed by Outpost.</dd>
            <dt>Litestream</dt>
            <dd>Continuous SQLite replication to a remote replica, so a database on one server is never the only copy.</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">A server is set up by a command, not a wiki page.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/packages/outpost/">Outpost</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

